In .NET MAUI, you hook into platform lifecycle events using the `ConfigureLifecycleEvents` method in your `MauiProgram.cs`:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Maui.LifecycleEvents;

public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
{
    var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
    builder.UseMauiApp<App>();

    builder.ConfigureLifecycleEvents(events =>
    {
#if ANDROID
        events.AddAndroid(android => android
            .OnPause(activity => { /* app pausing */ })
            .OnStop(activity => { /* app stopped */ }));
#elif IOS
        events.AddiOS(ios => ios
            .DidEnterBackground(app => { /* app backgrounded */ }));
#endif
    });

    return builder.Build();
}
```

**Key points:**

- Use `#if ANDROID` / `#elif IOS` preprocessor directives to keep platform code conditional.
- Android exposes `OnPause`, `OnStop`, `OnResume`, `OnDestroy`, etc.
- iOS exposes `DidEnterBackground`, `WillEnterForeground`, `WillTerminate`, etc.
- Each callback gives you the native object (`Activity` on Android, `UIApplication` on iOS) for further platform work.
